# Everything the frontend reads. Copy to .env.local for development; set these as
# BUILD-TIME variables on the hosting platform for a deployment.
#
# Only one variable, and one rule about it.

# Where the API lives. No trailing slash — src/lib/api.ts strips one, but every
# path it appends already starts with '/'.
#
# Local development: leave as-is, the API runs on 8001 (8000 is taken).
# Deployment: the API service's public URL, e.g. https://api-xxxx.up.railway.app
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# NEXT_PUBLIC_* IS INLINED AT BUILD TIME, NOT READ AT RUNTIME.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# `next build` substitutes the literal value into the JavaScript bundle. Setting
# this only as a runtime variable leaves the localhost fallback compiled in, and
# the deployed dashboard then calls http://localhost:8001 from the operator's own
# browser — which fails everywhere except the machine running the API locally,
# with a CORS or connection-refused error that looks nothing like a missing
# environment variable.
#
# On Railway: set it on the frontend service before the first deploy, and
# REDEPLOY after ever changing it. A restart is not enough; the bundle has to be
# rebuilt. The value is public either way — it ends up in JavaScript the browser
# downloads — so it must never hold a secret.
NEXT_PUBLIC_API_BASE=http://localhost:8001
